  "body": "Right now there is a really poor hack to make a `<table>` responsive, for example:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\">\r\n  <table>\r\n    ...\r\n  </table>\r\n</div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere are so many things wrong with this method.\r\n\r\n1. Creates an extra dom tree layer for no good reason.\r\n\r\n2. Only displays a horizontal slider at the bottom, which is totally useless when you have a huge table and you have to scroll from the top of the table all the way down the web page just to slide the table and then go back to the top.\r\n\r\nIs there anyway, you could address this issue and create a proper attribute that makes the `<table>` element responsive.\r\n\r\nOr do I need to contact W3c or the Google Chrome developers for them to create a working proposal?",
